# giotto-suite/Giotto

> R package to process, analyze and visualize spatial multi-omics data at all scales.

## The problem
Spatial technologies (Visium, Xenium, MERFISH, CODEX) produce very different formats and resolutions that must be analyzed together.

## What it actually does
Giotto Suite imports the data of each technology through `convenience_*` functions, then filters, normalizes, reduces dimensions and clusters cells. Spatial analysis looks for genes and interactions; some algorithms (Leiden) go through Python scripts. The README points to a site of documentation, examples and tutorials.

## How it is wired
```mermaid
flowchart TD
  gen["R/convenience_general.R"] --> fil["R/filter.R"]
  xen["R/convenience_xenium.R"] --> gen
  fil --> nor["R/normalize.R"]
  nor --> dim["R/dimension_reduction.R"]
  dim --> clu["R/clustering.R"]
  clu --> py["inst/python/python_leiden.py"]
  clu --> sg["R/spatial_genes.R"]
```

## Trying it
```r
pak::pkg_install("giotto-suite/Giotto")
```

## Cost and gotchas
Free. It requires R; some algorithms need Python, and spatial datasets are memory-heavy (not specified by the README). A license is present but not identified by GitHub.

## What it is not
It is not a general-purpose data science tool: it targets spatial biology. The README gives few code examples; everything is on the site.

## Alternatives
No alternative named in the README.

## For you
Worth watching if you work with spatial genomics in R; outside that field, it is of no interest.
